// `panic(why)` — the one the other three are written in terms of.
//
// Two things this fixture pins. The message goes to **stderr**, so a program's stdout stays the
// program's output and a dying message does not corrupt whatever is downstream of the pipe. And the
// exit code is **70**, the same code the runtime uses for a failed `requires` or an overflow: a
// shell should not have to tell "the program asserted something false" apart from "the language
// caught something false". The prefix `burxt panic:` is what distinguishes them, for a reader.
use "../../lib/result.bx";

region main {
    print("the output so far");
    panic("the ledger did not balance");
    print("this line is never reached");
}
